{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And he shall bear her iniquity</b> — This enters under her, i.e., in her place. We learn that one who causes a stumbling to his fellow enters in his stead for all punishments. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 30:16) from Sifrei (Matot 30).\n\nIt appears that this woman is inadvertent or misled, for Scripture speaks of a husband who heard and the wife did not know of it, and after some time he annulled and said to her that it was the day he heard. Scripture teaches us two things: that the husband bears iniquity as though he himself had vowed and profaned his word, and that she is exempt — there is upon her no punishment of inadvertent sins at all. But if the wife knows that he did not annul on the day he heard and is expert in this law, then she is liable, and also the husband does not cause the stumbling, but his punishment is like one who is able to annul and did not annul.\n\nScripture mentioned this regarding the husband, and the same law applies to the father, but Scripture spoke of the present case, for the father will be careful about this out of love for his daughter, whereas the husband may perhaps hate her and intend to bring guilt upon her.\n\nRabbi Avraham wrote: 'And he shall bear her iniquity' — because she is in his domain. If so, the meaning of the verse would be about coercing her to do what she forbade upon herself, and this is not correct:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "In the phrase 'but his punishment is like one who is able to annul and did not annul,' the subject appears to shift from the husband (who failed to annul) back to the husband's punishment; the logic is strained but the translation is literal."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:44.946835086Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}